{"word": "eternity", "source": "freedictionaryapi", "data": {"word": "eternity", "entries": [{"language": {"code": "en", "name": "English"}, "partOfSpeech": "noun", "pronunciations": [{"type": "ipa", "text": "/ɪˈtɜːnɪti/", "tags": ["Received Pronunciation"]}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "/əˈtɜɹnəti/", "tags": ["General American"]}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "/ɪ-/", "tags": ["General American"]}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "[-ɾi]", "tags": ["General American"]}], "forms": [{"word": "eternities", "tags": ["plural"]}, {"word": "æternitie", "tags": ["alternative", "obsolete"]}, {"word": "æternity", "tags": ["alternative", "obsolete"]}], "senses": [{"definition": "(uncountable) Existence without end, infinite time.", "tags": ["uncountable"], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "Eternity has generally been considered as divisible into two parts; which have been termed, eternity a parte ante, and eternity a parte post: that is, in plain English, that eternity which is past, and that eternity which is to come.", "reference": "1829, John Wesley, Sermons on Several Occasions, 10th edition, volume 2, Sermon LVIII: On the Eternity of God, page 1:"}, {"text": "This theory regards creation as an act of God in eternity past.", "reference": "1886, Augustus Hopkins Strong, Systematic Theology: a Compendium and Commonplace-book Designed for the Use of Theological Students, page 190:"}, {"text": "Those who like not the company of the saints on earth will get none of it in eternity; but, as godless company is their delight now, they will afterwards get enough of it, when they have eternity to pass in the roaring and blaspheming society of devils and reprobates in hell.", "reference": "2000, Thomas Boston, Human Nature in Its Fourfold State, page 247:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [{"language": {"code": "alt", "name": "Southern Altai"}, "word": "мӧҥкӱлик"}, {"language": {"code": "ar", "name": "Arabic"}, "word": "أَبَد"}, {"language": {"code": "hy", "name": "Armenian"}, "wor