ch is simply another form of expressing the same idea, and \"pass through time into eternity.\" in other words, time is a short period allotted to man in his probationary state—and we use the word time in contradistinction to the word eternity, merely for the accommodation of man in his finite sphere, that we may comprehend and learn to measure periods.", "reference": "1879, Erastus Snow, “Rest Signifies Change, etc.”, in Brigham Young, editor, Journal of Discourses, volume 21, published 1881:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(countable) A period of time which extends infinitely far into the future.", "tags": ["countable"], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "Every niche was filled by a funeral urn, and by marble shapes that bent down in a pale eternity of sorrow.", "reference": "1837, L[etitia] E[lizabeth] L[andon], “The Marriage”, in Ethel Churchill: Or, The Two Brides. […], volume I, London: Henry Colburn, […], →OCLC, page 220:"}, {"text": "No limit / Not none that I can see / No limit / Not one, not one / Not one for you or me / No limit / Well, it can go until eternity, yeah / No limits to our love, girl", "reference": "1978, Breakwater, “No Limit”, in Breakwater:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(metaphysics) The remainder of time that elapses after death.", "tags": ["countable", "uncountable"], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "When the wind blows not, where, then, is the wind? / Or when thou art not living, where art thou? / What should the wind care for the hours of calm or thou for death? / Thy life is long, Eternity is short. / So short that, shouldst thou die and Eternity should pass, and after the passing of Eternity thou shouldst live again, thou wouldst say: ‘I closed mine eyes but for an instant.’", "reference": "1905, Lord Dunsany [i.e., Edward Plunkett, 18th Baron of Dunsany], “Pegāna”, in The Gods of Pegāna, London: [Charles] Elkin Mathews, […], →OCLC, pages 75–76:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(informal, hyperbolic) A comparatively long time.", "tags": ["countable", "excessive", "informal", "uncountable"], "examples": ["It's been an eternity since we last saw each other."], "quotes": [{"text": "I had to wait in the station for ten days - an eternity.", "reference": "1899 February, Joseph Conrad, “The Heart of Darkness”, in Blackwood’s Edinburgh Magazine, volume CLXV, number M, New York, N.Y.: The Leonard Scott Publishing Company, […], →OCLC, part I, page 208:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}], "synonyms": ["atemporality", "eternal now", "extratemporality", "all time", "perpetuity", "evermore", "forever", "foreverhood", "afterlife", "an age", "ages", "centuries", "donkey's years", "hours", "a lifetime", "years", "yonks", "alwaysness", "eternality", "eternity", "foreverness"], "antonyms": ["sempiternity", "moment"]}], "source": {"url": "https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/eternity", "license": {"name": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/"}}}}